# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Never commit the filled-in copy —
# .gitignore allows this template through and nothing else.

# ── Groq (primary provider) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Several keys are pooled and used round-robin. When one hits its rate limit
# it is benched for as long as Groq asks and the next key takes over, so the
# keys behave like one larger quota.
#
# Use whichever form suits you — all three are read, and duplicates collapse.
# One key:
#GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_your_key_here
# A list (comma- or whitespace-separated) — usually the easiest for several:
#GROQ_API_KEYS=gsk_first,gsk_second,gsk_third
# Or numbered, which is handy in dashboards that only take one value per row:
#GROQ_API_KEY_1=gsk_first
#GROQ_API_KEY_2=gsk_second

# Optional. Leads the model chain; the built-in list stays as fallback so a
# decommissioned model degrades instead of breaking the feature.
#GROQ_MODEL=

# Optional. Point the pool at a proxy or a compatible endpoint.
#GROQ_BASE_URL=

# ── Vercel AI Gateway (used only if the Groq pool is empty or exhausted) ──
#AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
#ASTRAL_MODEL=

# With neither configured the app still answers: /api/chat falls back to a
# deterministic reader that works straight from your chart data.

# ── Diagnostics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unlocks GET /api/providers in production, which reports pool health: how
# many keys back the app, four characters of each, how close each is to
# exhaustion, and the last upstream error. That is a usable map of when and
# how to exhaust the pool, so it is sealed unless this is set.
#
# Leave it unset and the endpoint 404s in production. It stays open on
# localhost either way. Use a long random value:
#   node -e "console.log(crypto.randomUUID())"
#ASTRAL_DIAGNOSTICS_TOKEN=

# Read it with:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $ASTRAL_DIAGNOSTICS_TOKEN" https://…/api/providers
